% Query minimisation — every rule here is a conjunctive query, and
% containment.py decides which ones carry redundant atoms.
%
%   python3 containment.py programs/14-minimise.dl
%
% Redundancy is not a syntactic property: you cannot see it by looking
% for repeated atoms.  It is "some atom's job is already done by
% another, under a renaming of the non-head variables" — which is a
% homomorphism question, and the reason Chandra and Merlin (1977) is an
% optimiser result rather than a curiosity.

edge(a, b).
edge(b, c).

% 1. The self-join that isn't: e(X, Z) demands X have *an* outgoing
%    edge, which e(X, Y) already guarantees.  Minimises.
has_edge(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y), edge(X, Z).

% 2. Two hops, plus a spurious third atom folding onto the first.
two_hop(X) :- edge(X, Y), edge(Y, Z), edge(X, W).

% 3. A 2-cycle: neither atom can fold onto the other without moving a
%    head variable.  Already minimal.
mutual(X) :- edge(X, Y), edge(Y, X).

% 4. A triangle: minimal for the same reason, one size up.
triangle(X) :- edge(X, Y), edge(Y, Z), edge(Z, X).
